eliberately small Cortex-owned control kernel and mature external runtime/tooling substrates.\n\nV6 owns only the semantics that must remain portable and authoritative across providers:\n- authoritative task/work lifecycle state;\n- exact requirement/work-unit/generation identity;\n- scope/authority grants;\n- task/risk/agent-topology routing;\n- `AssurancePlan` obligations;\n- normalized evidence binding;\n- retry / blocked / verify / complete transitions.\n\nV6 does **not** become an agent-hosting cloud, model gateway, theorem prover, mutation framework, knowledge graph, CI system, or vendor-specific runtime.\n\n## Locked substrate direction\n\n### Primary development runtime\nUse **Microsoft Agent Framework** first because it is an MIT-licensed local/open-source development substrate that can support the V6 research path without forcing managed-cloud inference/runtime spend.\n\n### Portability requirement\nV6 must depend on a runtime-neutral contract rather than Microsoft/Azure objects. A later **AWS AgentCore** adapter must be possible without rewriting Cortex authoritative state or lifecycle semantics.\n\nVendor sessions, IDs, IAM objects, managed identities, gateways, and traces are execution resources, not authoritative Cortex state.\n\n### Model access\nKeep **LiteLLM** as the model/provider boundary so model seating/ranking and provider choice remain independent of the runtime substrate.\n\n### Durable knowledge\nKeep **FOSSIL** as the durable knowledge/evidence/provenance/lineage subsystem. Its knowledge graph and retrieval projections may inform bounded context and assurance relevance, but they do not own active Cortex task state. Live project artifacts remain authoritative for current project state.\n\n### Assurance\nTreat Pytest, Hypothesis/PBT, mutation testing, BDD, TLA+/TLC, theorem provers, benchmarks, chaos/fault injection, security analysis, critiques, and holdouts as external assurance providers. V6 decides which obligations are required and whether returned evidence is admissible for the exact work generation.\n\n### Repository/merge authority\nKeep GitHub/CI/branch protection as an independent repository merge boundary. V6 completion evidence must not silently bypass project-required checks.\n\n## Ownership split\n\n### Project owns WHAT must be true\n- requirements and constraints;\n- current source/tests/config;\n- acceptance criteria;\n- ADRs and architecture constraints;\n- SLO/performance/security requirements;\n- project-specific benchmarks;\n- human decisions.\n\n### V6 owns CONTROL\n- state;\n- scope;\n- routing;\n- assurance obligations;\n- evidence binding;\n- lifecycle transitions.\n\n### FOSSIL owns KNOWLEDGE\n- durable evidence;\n- provenance;\n- lineage;\n- historical/reusable knowledge;\n- source-quality metadata;\n- disagreement/supersession;\n- knowledge-graph/retrieval projections.\n\n### Runtime substrate owns EXECUTION INFRASTRUCTURE\n- session/runtime mechanics;\n- sandbox/isolation where provided;\n- tool transport;\n- runtime identity/credentials;\n- telemetry/traces;\n- vendor-specific policy enforcement.\n\n### LiteLLM owns MODEL TRANSPORT\n- provider protocol/access;\n- provider routing plumbing;\n- model endpoint abstraction.\n\n### Assurance providers own METHOD-SPECIFIC EXECUTION\nThey produce evidence; they do not directly mutate Cortex lifecycle state.\n\n## Anti-circular bootstrap rule\nThe external engineering pipeline must be able to qualify the V6 kernel before V6 itself is trusted to orchestrate project assurance. V6 cannot prove itself merely by asking V6 to say it passed.\n\n## Falsification rule\nThis decision does not grant V6 unlimited complexity. V6 must beat a serious conventional baseline (`coding agent + project CI/review`) on consequential failures or the additional mechanism is not justified.\n\n## Planned work packages\nCreate/expand tracked issues for:\n1. runtime-neutral `AgentRuntime`/runtime-provider contract and portability invariant;\n2. Microsoft Agent Framework qualification as the first substrate;\n3. AWS AgentCore adapter/portability qualification later;\n4. project-vs-V6-vs-FOSSIL ownership contracts;\n5. `AssurancePlan` / `AssuranceProvider` / `EvidenceReceipt` contracts;\n6. V6 kernel assurance profile using PBT, mutation, TLA+, proof candidates, fault tests and system composition tests;\n7. agent topology/routing (worker/planner/researcher/reviewer/assurance/evaluator) as roles over common primitives rather than separate products;\n8. live-project vs FOSSIL vs hybrid context bakeoff;\n9. V6-vs-conventional-pipeline automated benchmark/kill criterion.\n\n## Acceptance\n- [ ] Durable repo documentation records this locked execution plan and ownership split.\n- [ ] Roadmap links the corresponding tracked work packages.\n- [ ] The Microsoft-first/AWS-portable runtime direction is explicit.\n- [ ] No vendor-specific object is designated authoritative Cortex state.\n- [ ] LiteLLM, FOSSIL, assurance tools and GitHub/CI remain separate ownership boundaries.\n- [ ] Future detailed issue expansion can resume from repository state without relying on this chat transcript.\n\n## Existing dependency floor\n#2 -> #3 -> #4/#7 remain the first implementation proof sequence. This issue expands the path after the minimal kernel earns continuation.","closed_by":null,"reactions":{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/Pukujan/Cortex-v6/issues/9/reactions","total_count":0,"+1":0,"-1":0,"laugh":0,"hooray":0,"confused":0,"heart":0,"rocket":0,"eyes":0},"timeline_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/Pukujan/Cortex-v6/issues/9/timeline","performed_via_github_app":{"id":1144995,"client_id":"Iv23liVemv8A9if9v0F2","slug":"chatgpt-codex-connector","node_id":"A_kwHOAOQ6Gs4AEXij","owner":{"login":"openai","id":14957082,"node_id":"MDEyOk9yZ2FuaXphdGlvbjE0OTU3MDgy","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/14957082?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/openai","html_url":"https://github.com/openai","followers_url